["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"ess","stream_identity":"01a0d3e0-4003-757b-b72c-9441fdf06fca","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"## Goal\n\n`ess verify crosswalk`: hold a set of names that live outside the specification — the messages, enums, rpcs and oneof arms of a `.proto`; the `METHOD /path` keys of a REST inventory; the channels of a push inventory — to what the system declares, and refuse a gap. Output is the mapping table (name ↔ declaration by `naming.wire` or by an authored `UNMAPPED:` reason), deterministic, exit 1 on any name without a row, any row whose declaration does not exist, any row whose key is not the declaration's wire, and any name whose only explanation is a placeholder.\n\n## Why (evidence, 2026-09-10)\n\nA consumer carries this as ~500 lines of Python beside its specification because ESS has no verb for it:\n- `an internal application repository` `the internal specification repository/scripts/gen_crosswalk.py` + `check_crosswalk.py` + `test_check_crosswalk.py` (SPEC-C2, commit `cb8315a`; extended in wave 2026-09-10-2 for a second source): 189 proto names (`crosswalk/proto.md`), 36 REST keys + 7 Faye channels (`crosswalk/backend.md`) ↔ 155 declarations of one domain, run in `task contract:check` and CI.\n- Two adversarial passes on that hand-rolled checker found three defects the verb would not have: a stale row survived a `naming.wire` rename (names compared, wires not); a key mentioned in prose counted as a row; a key with no wire match and no authored reason was written as a placeholder `UNMAPPED` row and the check passed (`review-result:adversary-backend-domain-in-ess-pass-1` in that repository's store, findings 11-13).\n- The same consumer's proto crosswalk records, per row, the ESS ↔ proto type pair SPEC-D1 (protobuf binding) will need; a verb that owns the table owns that record.\n\nIt is `verify`, not `generate`: the table is a by-product; the refusal is the deliverable (\"hold an implementation … to what a system says\"). `generate project` and `verify conform` are neighbours, not homes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] `ess verify crosswalk --path <system> --source <kind>=<file> [--source …] --reasons <authored.yaml> --out <dir>` where `kind` ∈ at least `proto` (a `FileDescriptorSet` or a `.proto` parsed by the existing protobuf support), `list` (one name per line — the inventory case), `openapi` (paths × methods). Each source's names are listed by the adapter, never by regex over prose.\n- [ ] A row per source name: the declaration whose `naming.wire` (or declared name, for proto) equals it, else the authored `UNMAPPED:` reason from `--reasons`, else **refusal** (no placeholder rows).\n- [ ] Refuses: an unexplained name; a row whose declaration is absent from the resolved IR; a row whose key ≠ the declaration's wire; an authored reason for a name no source lists (stale reasons).\n- [ ] Byte-identical output on two runs; the table carries the model digest like `ess-docs/1` pages, so `verify impact` can name it as invalidated.\n- [ ] `ess verify impact` lists the crosswalk among invalidated artifacts when a declaration's wire changes.\n- [ ] Design page under `docs/design/` first (AGENTS.md: a construct is a design page before it is code), naming the source adapters and the reasons-file format.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nBinding generation (protobuf projection, SPEC-D1's need) — the crosswalk records the pair, the binding consumes it later. Reading a source repository's code (a `downstream.go`) — that stays a consumer-side inventory; the verb takes the inventory's name list.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Filed from the the internal application repository wave coordinator at the operator's request (\"should we not add crosswalk functionality into ESS itself?\"), 2026-09-10. Epic not chosen: `epic:review-boundary-remediation` (\"Preserve ESS guarantees across boundaries\") is the nearest; a source outside the specification is a boundary. Relate it if that reading holds.\n- The consumer's generator (`gen_crosswalk.py`, Python, ~230 lines) is the reference behaviour for the proto adapter, including its `MANUAL`/`RPC`/`ENUM`/`ARM_NOTE` name classes.\n","document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"ent-f392557d99137e186143ae7507197367","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/crosswalk-verb-external-names-held-to-declarations","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":10,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"## Goal\n\n`ess verify crosswalk`: hold a set of names that live outside the specification — the messages, enums, rpcs and oneof arms of a `.proto`; the `METHOD /path` keys of a REST inventory; the channels of a push inventory — to what the system declares, and refuse a gap. Output is the mapping table (name ↔ declaration by `naming.wire` or by an authored `UNMAPPED:` reason), deterministic, exit 1 on any name without a row, any row whose declaration does not exist, any row whose key is not the declaration's wire, and any name whose only explanation is a placeholder.\n\n## Why (evidence, 2026-09-10)\n\nA consumer carries this as ~500 lines of Python beside its specification because ESS has no verb for it:\n- `an internal application repository` `the internal specification repository/scripts/gen_crosswalk.py` + `check_crosswalk.py` + `test_check_crosswalk.py` (SPEC-C2, commit `cb8315a`; extended in wave 2026-09-10-2 for a second source): 189 proto names (`crosswalk/proto.md`), 36 REST keys + 7 Faye channels (`crosswalk/backend.md`) ↔ 155 declarations of one domain, run in `task contract:check` and CI.\n- Two adversarial passes on that hand-rolled checker found three defects the verb would not have: a stale row survived a `naming.wire` rename (names compared, wires not); a key mentioned in prose counted as a row; a key with no wire match and no authored reason was written as a placeholder `UNMAPPED` row and the check passed (`review-result:adversary-backend-domain-in-ess-pass-1` in that repository's store, findings 11-13).\n- The same consumer's proto crosswalk records, per row, the ESS ↔ proto type pair SPEC-D1 (protobuf binding) will need; a verb that owns the table owns that record.\n\nIt is `verify`, not `generate`: the table is a by-product; the refusal is the deliverable (\"hold an implementation … to what a system says\"). `generate project` and `verify conform` are neighbours, not homes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] `ess verify crosswalk --path <system> --source <kind>=<file> [--source …] --reasons <authored.yaml> --out <dir>` where `kind` ∈ at least `proto` (a `FileDescriptorSet` or a `.proto` parsed by the existing protobuf support), `list` (one name per line — the inventory case), `openapi` (paths × methods). Each source's names are listed by the adapter, never by regex over prose.\n- [ ] A row per source name: the declaration whose `naming.wire` (or declared name, for proto) equals it, else the authored `UNMAPPED:` reason from `--reasons`, else **refusal** (no placeholder rows).\n- [ ] Refuses: an unexplained name; a row whose declaration is absent from the resolved IR; a row whose key ≠ the declaration's wire; an authored reason for a name no source lists (stale reasons).\n- [ ] Byte-identical output on two runs; the table carries the model digest like `ess-docs/1` pages, so `verify impact` can name it as invalidated.\n- [ ] `ess verify impact` lists the crosswalk among invalidated artifacts when a declaration's wire changes.\n- [ ] Design page under `docs/design/` first (AGENTS.md: a construct is a design page before it is code), naming the source adapters and the reasons-file format.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nBinding generation (protobuf projection, SPEC-D1's need) — the crosswalk records the pair, the binding consumes it later. Reading a source repository's code (a `downstream.go`) — that stays a consumer-side inventory; the verb takes the in